Subject: sandbox: allocate nothing in the user-namespace probe child

Credentials::CanCreateProcessInNewUserNS() probes with a raw clone(), which
runs none of libc's fork handling, and its child called
SetGidAndUidMaps(), whose KernelSupportsDenySetgroups() builds a
base::FilePath and so allocates. With musl's single heap lock, a child
cloned while another thread of the host process held that lock waits on it
for ever, and the host waits on the child: a Qt WebEngine host such as
plasmashell hangs at start. The child now writes setgroups, gid_map and
uid_map with open() and write() alone, and takes a missing
/proc/self/setgroups for a kernel that needs no deny, as
KernelSupportsDenySetgroups() does.

--- a/src/3rdparty/chromium/sandbox/linux/services/credentials.cc
+++ b/src/3rdparty/chromium/sandbox/linux/services/credentials.cc
@@ -288,11 +288,19 @@
   }
 
   // The parent process could have had threads. In the child, these threads
-  // have disappeared.
+  // have disappeared, and so has anything that would release a lock one of
+  // them held: ForkWithFlags() is a raw clone() that runs none of libc's fork
+  // handling, so the child holds a copy of the allocator's lock if any thread
+  // held it. musl's allocator has one lock for the whole heap, and a child
+  // that allocates then waits on it for ever while the parent waits on the
+  // child. Nothing below may allocate.
   if (pid == 0) {
     // unshare() requires the effective uid and gid to have a mapping in the
-    // parent namespace.
-    if (!SetGidAndUidMaps(gid, uid))
+    // parent namespace. A kernel without /proc/self/setgroups needs no deny.
+    if (!NamespaceUtils::DenySetgroups() && errno != ENOENT)
+      _exit(kExitFailure);
+    if (!NamespaceUtils::WriteToIdMapFile("/proc/self/gid_map", gid) ||
+        !NamespaceUtils::WriteToIdMapFile("/proc/self/uid_map", uid))
       _exit(kExitFailure);
 
     // Make sure we drop CAP_SYS_ADMIN.
